<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amelia, Umbria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ameria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amelia, the ancient Ameria, is a hill town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In antiquity it was one of the leading Umbrian communities and later a Roman municipium, and its massive polygonal walls, built of huge fitted stone blocks, still survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> defended Sextus Roscius of Ameria in a famous murder trial in 80 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amelia-umbria.jpg" alt="Amelia, Umbria" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mattana · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amelia_001.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amelia is a town and comune in the province of Terni, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, not far from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>. It stands on the slope of a hill at about 406 metres above sea level, between the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and that of the Nera, close to the point where the two rivers meet. It lies 24 kilometres from Terni and 13 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narni">Narni</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Cato, the city was founded 964 years before the Third Macedonian War, which corresponds to 1135 BCE. The date cannot be given certain historical value, but it shows the great age attributed to the place. Another tradition, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>, gives 1136 BCE and names either Amisio, said to be a king of the Aborigines, or a supposed princess of Veii as founder. The city later came under Etruscan influence. The opening of the Via Amerina in 240 BCE linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, and the town then began to take on an urban form.</p><p>Troops from Amelia fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s advance, and the town became a prosperous Roman municipium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> described it as a flourishing municipality whose fertile land reached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. Under Augustus its lands were divided among veterans, though it did not become a colony. Amelia is recorded as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat from the 4th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> later destroyed the city. In 731 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Liutprand seized it; in 1240 the troops of Frederick II sacked it. It remained tied to the Papal States until Piedmontese troops entered it on 21 September 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Amelia is almost entirely encircled by polygonal walls of the 6th to 4th centuries BCE, among the most complete of their type. Their limestone blocks, sometimes more than 2 metres long, are fitted without mortar, and eighteenth-century travellers attributed them to the Pelasgians. The town also keeps Roman cisterns of the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE, ten barrel-vaulted chambers cut in the rock, and a bronze statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> found outside the Porta Romana in 1963. Amelia is known for its dried figs, a product recorded since antiquity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amelia, Umbria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Umbrian communities were independent towns and hill sanctuaries, among them Iguvium, Tuder, Asisium, Mevania, Spoletium and <b>Ameria</b>, without a lasting central government.</blockquote><a href="/state/umbrians">Umbrians</a></li></ul></section>
